1
00:01:37,097 --> 00:01:40,761
- Here's the mail.
- Thank you.

2
00:01:43,503 --> 00:01:45,734
Um, Mr. Phillips.

3
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:49,905
- Something wrong?
- Uh, not exactly.

4
00:01:50,077 --> 00:01:51,511
See, my little sister,

5
00:01:52,179 --> 00:01:54,910
well, she graduates from high
school up in Richmond next Friday.

6
00:01:55,082 --> 00:01:56,414
So you wanna take Friday off.

7
00:01:57,584 --> 00:01:59,784
- That's all right with me, Jenny.
- Oh, heh, thank you.

8
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:01,411
Thank you so much.

9
00:02:01,588 --> 00:02:04,057
Assistant Commissioner Phillips,
may I have a word with you?

10
00:02:04,224 --> 00:02:06,489
- I'm sorry. Your name is?
- Carla Myers.

11
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,755
Councilwoman Carla Myers.
This will only take a minute.

12
00:02:10,430 --> 00:02:12,831
Close the door on your way out.

13
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:19,740
Tomorrow morning at 10,
I'm holding a press conference

14
00:02:20,307 --> 00:02:22,867
during which I will show
that you used this office

15
00:02:23,043 --> 00:02:26,241
to embezzle close to
$100,000 in public funds.

16
00:02:27,447 --> 00:02:29,143
I don't know what
you're talking about.

17
00:02:29,316 --> 00:02:30,909
I have proof.

18
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,780
Good God.

19
00:02:36,523 --> 00:02:39,425
I felt it only fair to warn you.

20
00:02:40,694 --> 00:02:44,290
- I was going to pay it back.
- Sure you were.

21
00:02:44,464 --> 00:02:48,333
I have a son in college and
two daughters in private school.

22
00:02:48,502 --> 00:02:51,165
I was temporarily short of cash.

23
00:02:51,338 --> 00:02:53,534
Save it for the TV
cameras, Mr. Phillips.

24
00:02:54,007 --> 00:02:57,637
Please, don't do this.

25
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:01,547
I'll resign, okay? Right
now. This afternoon.

26
00:03:01,715 --> 00:03:04,048
And I'll pay back every
penny that I took. I promise.

27
00:03:04,217 --> 00:03:06,709
Just don't go public with this.

28
00:03:06,887 --> 00:03:09,721
You won't be just destroying
me. You'll be destroying my family.

29
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:12,816
Don't try to make me
sound like the criminal here.

30
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:19,666
This isn't about what
I did, is it? Damn it.

31
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:22,359
This is about you. And all
the publicity you're gonna get.

32
00:03:22,536 --> 00:03:24,698
You're trying to make
a name for yourself.

33
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:27,397
What I did was wrong. But
what you're doing is despicable.

34
00:03:27,574 --> 00:03:30,373
Well, the people are going
to think differently, Mr. Phillips.

35
00:03:44,758 --> 00:03:47,694
Mm... Mm.

36
00:03:50,997 --> 00:03:53,466
Pour some little hot
sauce. Just a dab.

37
00:03:53,633 --> 00:03:55,602
Uh-uh, uh-uh, uh-uh,
Dad, that'll blow me up.

38
00:03:55,769 --> 00:03:58,295
Uh-uh. Whoa, whoa, whoa.

39
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:00,134
You'll sizzle a
little with this.

40
00:04:00,307 --> 00:04:02,970
I love the taste of this.

41
00:04:08,181 --> 00:04:09,706
Here. Here.

42
00:04:09,883 --> 00:04:11,083
Is it gonna be too hot for me?

43
00:04:11,251 --> 00:04:13,550
- Are you sure, dad?
- No, no, delicious.

44
00:04:22,796 --> 00:04:25,789
- There's Arthur.
- What?

45
00:04:25,966 --> 00:04:28,834
Guy who lives across
the street. Arthur.

46
00:04:30,437 --> 00:04:32,372
<i>Carla Myers accused this man,</i>

47
00:04:32,539 --> 00:04:36,032
<i>Arthur Phillips of
embezzling close to $100,000</i>

48
00:04:36,209 --> 00:04:37,677
<i>in the course of
his three year term</i>

49
00:04:37,844 --> 00:04:40,006
<i>as assistant commissioner
of transportation.</i>

50
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:42,547
Pfft. Well, there goes
the neighborhood.

51
00:04:42,716 --> 00:04:44,378
<i>Ms. Myers made the accusation</i>

52
00:04:44,551 --> 00:04:46,543
<i>at a press conference
held this morning</i>

53
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:48,951
<i>at her office at
the City Council.</i>

54
00:04:49,122 --> 00:04:50,988
<i>During my bid for election,</i>

55
00:04:51,158 --> 00:04:55,357
<i>I promised to rid city government
of waste and corruption.</i>

56
00:04:55,529 --> 00:05:00,297
<i>For years, grafts and payoffs
were the normal course of business.</i>

57
00:05:00,467 --> 00:05:05,235
<i>The people of this city are victims
of dealmakers and racketeers</i>

58
00:05:05,405 --> 00:05:10,139
<i>and I vow to bring that to an end
and I am keeping that promise.</i>

59
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:12,643
<i>Moreover, this is
only the beginning.</i>

60
00:05:12,813 --> 00:05:16,545
<i>I fully intend to continue
seeking out such abuses of power.</i>

61
00:05:42,142 --> 00:05:45,271
Let's hear it for the
tumbling traffic cops.

62
00:05:48,648 --> 00:05:52,915
Okay. Now it's time for
some music. A little rap music.

63
00:05:53,753 --> 00:05:57,588
Yes. Cops go in for rap too.
At least, these two guys are.

64
00:06:01,962 --> 00:06:04,090
Well, what do you want?

65
00:06:11,705 --> 00:06:13,105
Mm.

66
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,010
Sorry.

67
00:07:43,630 --> 00:07:46,225
That was Assistant District
Attorney Michael Brennen.

68
00:07:46,399 --> 00:07:47,423
Let's hear it.

69
00:07:59,346 --> 00:08:02,680
Uh, yeah. The
warrant's on its way.

70
00:08:02,849 --> 00:08:04,875
Uh, uh, right. Soon
as you do, call me.

71
00:08:06,252 --> 00:08:07,692
Don't you take weekends off?

72
00:08:07,854 --> 00:08:08,981
Look who's talking.

73
00:08:11,992 --> 00:08:14,689
Show for underprivileged
kids at the community center.

74
00:08:14,861 --> 00:08:17,353
Lieutenant Davies asked
me to do my clown routine.

75
00:08:17,530 --> 00:08:21,058
So, what's so important that
I had to miss my curtain call?

76
00:08:24,037 --> 00:08:26,734
Carla Myers was found
dead in her home this morning.

77
00:08:28,108 --> 00:08:31,806
- No. I just saw her on the news.
- Strangled. Her mother found her.

78
00:08:36,683 --> 00:08:38,982
Her watch and a bunch
of other jewelry is missing.

79
00:08:39,152 --> 00:08:43,021
So it may have been a
simple burglary gone south or...

80
00:08:43,189 --> 00:08:46,387
Maybe it was that fella that
she accused of embezzlement.

81
00:08:46,559 --> 00:08:47,652
And maybe he went bonkers.

82
00:08:47,827 --> 00:08:49,538
- What was his name again?
- Arthur Phillips.

83
00:08:49,562 --> 00:08:52,361
Detective Garrison's on his way
over there with a search warrant.

84
00:08:55,301 --> 00:09:00,035
This one's yours, Michael,
high profile, heat from all sides.

85
00:09:00,206 --> 00:09:02,641
It's right up your alley.
Make me look good.

86
00:09:03,810 --> 00:09:08,077
Always do. Uh, Garrison
know to report to me?

87
00:09:08,248 --> 00:09:10,365
I'll tell him when he
calls from Phillips.

88
00:09:10,389 --> 00:09:11,946
So I have time to change, hmm?

89
00:09:14,521 --> 00:09:16,114
- Please.
- Heh.

90
00:09:17,891 --> 00:09:19,689
Yowza.

91
00:09:24,130 --> 00:09:26,326
- There's Arthur.
- They're arresting him.

92
00:09:26,499 --> 00:09:27,865
Yeah.

93
00:09:32,572 --> 00:09:36,339
Ben, Ben. Ben,
you gotta help me.

94
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:39,104
They think I killed Carla Myers.
- In the car, please.

95
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:41,248
They found some
watch in my garage.

96
00:09:41,414 --> 00:09:43,679
- Jim Gaston. Right?
- That's right.

97
00:09:43,850 --> 00:09:45,045
What's going on?

98
00:09:45,218 --> 00:09:47,517
The watch we found
belonged to the victim.

99
00:09:47,687 --> 00:09:48,916
Her mother just ID'd it.

100
00:09:49,089 --> 00:09:51,285
I don't know how
it got there. I swear.

101
00:09:51,858 --> 00:09:54,692
In the car. Come
on. Watch your head.

102
00:09:54,861 --> 00:09:58,525
Ben, please help me.

103
00:10:13,012 --> 00:10:16,449
You did borrow his
hedge clippers last month.

104
00:10:16,616 --> 00:10:23,045
Yeah. But they're old and rusty,
dull. I was gonna take them back.

105
00:10:27,594 --> 00:10:33,261
No, no, no, that
ain't gonna cut it, pal.

106
00:10:33,433 --> 00:10:36,801
I want him in court and I want
him on the stand. You got that?

107
00:10:38,838 --> 00:10:39,862
It ain't locked.

108
00:10:41,307 --> 00:10:43,936
Well, then find him, because
let me tell you something, bub,

109
00:10:44,110 --> 00:10:47,205
if he's a no show, he
doesn't go on my list, you do.

110
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:49,372
You wanna be on my list?

111
00:10:50,717 --> 00:10:53,585
Okay. Now, you do that.

112
00:10:56,789 --> 00:11:00,783
Wonder of wonders.
It's Ben Matlock.

113
00:11:01,794 --> 00:11:05,162
Michael Brennen. Pleased to
meet you. Sit down. Sit down.

114
00:11:05,331 --> 00:11:08,893
- You sure this is a good time?
- Heh, public defender.

115
00:11:09,068 --> 00:11:11,697
Makes him feel all self-righteous
when I yell at him like that.

116
00:11:11,871 --> 00:11:14,500
- Gets him right up off his duff.
- It's an act, huh?

117
00:11:14,974 --> 00:11:16,909
I hear you're something
of a showman yourself.

118
00:11:17,076 --> 00:11:18,977
I don't know.

119
00:11:19,846 --> 00:11:23,783
I don't have anything
like this in my office.

120
00:11:24,984 --> 00:11:26,509
I love theatrical things.

121
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:29,622
They remind me that people
aren't always what they seem.

122
00:11:29,789 --> 00:11:32,657
- Yeah. You from New York?
- Chicago.

123
00:11:33,159 --> 00:11:37,221
- How'd you wind up here?
- I'm an old friend of Jerry Carlisle's.

124
00:11:37,397 --> 00:11:38,990
When he heard about...

125
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:42,540
When he heard that I
was thinking about moving,

126
00:11:42,702 --> 00:11:44,542
he suggested I come
down here and work for him.

127
00:11:44,704 --> 00:11:46,935
- What'd you do up there?
- I was a defense attorney.

128
00:11:47,106 --> 00:11:50,235
- Just like you.
- You know what?

129
00:11:50,410 --> 00:11:53,574
I used to be an
assistant DA, just like you.

130
00:11:56,049 --> 00:11:59,019
- How about that?
- Yeah. Yeah.

131
00:11:59,185 --> 00:12:03,179
I understand, uh, you're
prosecuting the Arthur Phillips case.

132
00:12:03,723 --> 00:12:05,555
Does that mean you're
representing him?

133
00:12:06,426 --> 00:12:08,861
He's a friend, lives
across the street.

134
00:12:09,028 --> 00:12:11,259
I'm always borrowing
his tools and stuff.

135
00:12:11,431 --> 00:12:14,026
He never locks his garage.

136
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:16,567
Anybody could have
planted that watch there.

137
00:12:17,704 --> 00:12:19,195
Yeah. Maybe.

138
00:12:19,372 --> 00:12:22,706
But I'm betting that Phillips put
that watch in that tool box, Ben.

139
00:12:22,875 --> 00:12:25,140
On his way from the car
to the door to the house.

140
00:12:25,311 --> 00:12:27,837
It was in his path.

141
00:12:28,014 --> 00:12:30,040
You hungry? I'm hungry.
You like hot dogs?

142
00:12:31,584 --> 00:12:34,349
- Yeah.
- Heh, well, let's do lunch.

143
00:12:35,455 --> 00:12:38,186
- You cook here?
- Yeah. Sleep in here, too, sometimes.

144
00:12:38,358 --> 00:12:40,350
The bed pulls out.

145
00:12:40,526 --> 00:12:42,017
How does your wife like that?

146
00:12:43,663 --> 00:12:44,722
My wife's dead.

147
00:12:46,733 --> 00:12:47,860
So is mine.

148
00:12:53,539 --> 00:12:56,441
You know, Ben, forensics
found Phillips' fingerprints

149
00:12:56,609 --> 00:13:00,205
all over the Myers house.
Are you aware of that?

150
00:13:00,380 --> 00:13:02,940
Yeah. He went over there
the night before the murder

151
00:13:03,116 --> 00:13:06,052
to try to talk her out of
that press conference.

152
00:13:06,219 --> 00:13:10,748
- Anybody see him there?
- Well, no.

153
00:13:10,923 --> 00:13:12,585
Can anybody vouch
for his whereabouts

154
00:13:12,759 --> 00:13:14,387
on the day of the murder?

155
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:17,257
- Could you get that flame for me?
- Oh.

156
00:13:18,031 --> 00:13:20,626
- Thanks.
- Yeah. He was in his car.

157
00:13:21,267 --> 00:13:23,168
He drove around all
day and half the night

158
00:13:23,336 --> 00:13:25,362
because he was too
ashamed to see anybody.

159
00:13:25,538 --> 00:13:29,942
Oh, no, he wasn't,
Ben. He was mad as hell.

160
00:13:30,109 --> 00:13:33,238
So he broke into her
house, strangled her,

161
00:13:33,413 --> 00:13:38,283
tried to make it look like a
robbery by ransacking the house,

162
00:13:38,451 --> 00:13:43,651
stealing her watch and then
he hid the watch in his garage,

163
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:45,553
intending to
dispose of it later.

164
00:13:49,262 --> 00:13:52,232
Only way to chop onions. Okay.

165
00:13:52,398 --> 00:13:57,598
Here's how you make your
authentic Chicago-style hot dogs.

166
00:13:57,770 --> 00:14:03,573
You get your Vienna
natural casing hot dogs.

167
00:14:04,677 --> 00:14:07,146
You get your Mary Anns.

168
00:14:07,313 --> 00:14:10,010
- Your what?
- Your buns.

169
00:14:10,183 --> 00:14:12,379
Your Mary Anns.

170
00:14:14,921 --> 00:14:17,481
I have them shipped
in from Chicago.

171
00:14:18,057 --> 00:14:19,577
You get your buns.
You get your onions.

172
00:14:19,726 --> 00:14:23,060
But the most important
thing that you get is

173
00:14:25,598 --> 00:14:30,662
your tiny hot sport peppers.

174
00:14:45,017 --> 00:14:47,680
Mm. Boy, that pepper's hot.

175
00:14:47,854 --> 00:14:50,323
Mm, but good.

176
00:14:50,490 --> 00:14:53,119
I'll tell you the truth. That's
the best hot dog I ever had.

177
00:14:53,292 --> 00:14:56,956
Heh, we're so much
alike, it's scary, Ben.

178
00:14:57,430 --> 00:14:59,194
You could be my evil twin.

179
00:14:59,365 --> 00:15:00,799
How many cases have you lost?

180
00:15:02,101 --> 00:15:04,866
A few here and
there. How about you?

181
00:15:06,873 --> 00:15:09,172
As a prosecutor? None.

182
00:15:12,545 --> 00:15:14,411
Let me ask you something.

183
00:15:14,580 --> 00:15:16,708
Doesn't it bother you

184
00:15:17,517 --> 00:15:20,885
that Arthur's fingerprints were
found all over the scene of the crime

185
00:15:21,053 --> 00:15:22,077
but none on the watch?

186
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:24,575
I mean, why would he wipe the
watch clean and nothing else?

187
00:15:24,724 --> 00:15:27,421
I don't know. Maybe he
wasn't thinking straight,

188
00:15:27,593 --> 00:15:29,391
because he had just
committed murder.

189
00:15:35,501 --> 00:15:37,060
Brennen.

190
00:15:37,970 --> 00:15:39,905
I'll be right there.

191
00:15:41,374 --> 00:15:43,707
The police have been
talking to, uh, a guy

192
00:15:43,876 --> 00:15:47,608
that drives a bus past Carla
Myers' house every day.

193
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:50,948
- Ah, you might wanna come with me.
- Hmm.

194
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:09,965
Now, face front again, please.

195
00:16:16,008 --> 00:16:18,239
Yeah. Um...

196
00:16:19,979 --> 00:16:23,313
Second guy up from
the left. That's him.

197
00:16:23,483 --> 00:16:25,315
- You sure?
- Yeah.

198
00:16:25,485 --> 00:16:27,565
That's the guy I saw running
out of that lady's house

199
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:28,779
the day she got murdered.

200
00:16:28,955 --> 00:16:32,187
Ahem, I'm positive.

201
00:16:53,212 --> 00:16:57,775
And then I saw him run around the
side of the house toward the street.

202
00:16:57,950 --> 00:17:00,419
Now, why would you
remember something like that?

203
00:17:00,586 --> 00:17:02,851
I mean, haven't you ever
seen a guy running before?

204
00:17:03,022 --> 00:17:05,821
Not a guy his age and
not dressed like he was.

205
00:17:06,492 --> 00:17:09,724
I also remember because I thought
the was gonna run out in front of me.

206
00:17:09,896 --> 00:17:11,660
But he didn't.

207
00:17:11,831 --> 00:17:14,562
He turned and went running down
the sidewalk in the other direction.

208
00:17:14,734 --> 00:17:17,704
- And that was at what time?
- Six eighteen.

209
00:17:17,870 --> 00:17:21,671
Six eighteen? Are you telling
me that at that exact moment,

210
00:17:21,841 --> 00:17:23,833
you just happened
to look at your watch?

211
00:17:24,010 --> 00:17:25,979
No. Of course not, I
remembered what time it was,

212
00:17:26,145 --> 00:17:28,239
or about what time it was,

213
00:17:28,414 --> 00:17:32,374
because I was three blocks away
from my 6:20 stop at Vine and Watson

214
00:17:33,219 --> 00:17:36,986
and I was running right
on schedule, just as usual.

215
00:17:37,156 --> 00:17:39,819
And you're positive
that the guy that you saw

216
00:17:39,992 --> 00:17:41,592
was the guy you
picked out of the lineup.

217
00:17:41,761 --> 00:17:42,922
Absolutely.

218
00:17:43,095 --> 00:17:46,532
Oh, come on, Mr. White,
you're a bus driver.

219
00:17:46,699 --> 00:17:48,531
Since when is having
a photographic memory

220
00:17:48,701 --> 00:17:50,602
in the job description
for bus drivers?

221
00:17:52,071 --> 00:17:54,870
I was robbed at
gunpoint last year

222
00:17:55,041 --> 00:18:00,412
by somebody who got away with it
because I couldn't remember his face.

223
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:04,608
Believe me, since I have made it a
point to remember everybody's face

224
00:18:04,784 --> 00:18:07,913
and I am sure that's who I saw.

225
00:18:10,056 --> 00:18:11,490
Good.

226
00:18:12,325 --> 00:18:13,725
Very good.

227
00:18:13,893 --> 00:18:16,294
- Any questions, counselor?
- No.

228
00:18:16,462 --> 00:18:21,332
- I think you've about hit on everything.
- Thank you, Mr. White. I'll be in touch.

229
00:18:23,469 --> 00:18:26,303
Well, you don't need
my help on this one.

230
00:18:28,541 --> 00:18:30,908
- Uh, you got new carpeting.
- Mm-hm, yeah.

231
00:18:31,077 --> 00:18:33,637
I never got new carpeting
when this was my office.

232
00:18:35,014 --> 00:18:36,346
How come you got new carpeting?

233
00:18:36,515 --> 00:18:39,178
I made Griswold over
in Buildings and Grounds

234
00:18:39,352 --> 00:18:41,344
a hot dog one night.

235
00:18:41,520 --> 00:18:43,785
Next day, new carpeting.

236
00:18:45,424 --> 00:18:47,984
- He's something, huh, Ben?
- Yeah, heh.

237
00:18:56,769 --> 00:19:00,536
I'm telling you I wasn't there. He
couldn't have possibly seen me.

238
00:19:00,706 --> 00:19:03,437
- All right.
- The man's lying, Ben.

239
00:19:04,010 --> 00:19:06,343
Mistaken, maybe. I
don't know about lying.

240
00:19:06,512 --> 00:19:08,538
What's the difference?
Because of him...

241
00:19:08,714 --> 00:19:11,240
I don't have a prayer now, do I?

242
00:19:12,518 --> 00:19:16,956
Don't say it. Just do
something. Please.

243
00:19:39,478 --> 00:19:41,879
- Oh, hi. Heh, sorry.
- Oh.

244
00:19:42,048 --> 00:19:45,746
I don't know anyone else was
here. I hope I didn't startle you.

245
00:19:45,918 --> 00:19:50,049
Michael Brennen,
assistant district attorney.

246
00:19:50,489 --> 00:19:54,893
Aha. I'm Leanne Mclntyre,
Ben Matlock's daughter.

247
00:19:56,328 --> 00:19:59,264
- Ah.
- Heh.

248
00:19:59,432 --> 00:20:02,766
- So, what are you doing here?
- I always check out the crime scene.

249
00:20:02,935 --> 00:20:04,335
- Really?
- Uh-huh.

250
00:20:05,371 --> 00:20:07,897
Gives me a feel
for what happened,

251
00:20:08,074 --> 00:20:11,101
what the killer did,
what the victim did,

252
00:20:11,677 --> 00:20:15,580
how the victim must
have felt, especially that.

253
00:20:15,748 --> 00:20:18,274
I never wanna forget
how the victim felt.

254
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:23,511
I know people like your
dad, they defend the accused,

255
00:20:23,689 --> 00:20:25,783
guilty or innocent.

256
00:20:25,958 --> 00:20:31,795
I like to think I defend
the victim, alive or dead.

257
00:20:33,199 --> 00:20:38,069
I speak for her,
I fight for her.

258
00:20:39,672 --> 00:20:42,540
I make sure she
doesn't get forgotten.

259
00:20:47,546 --> 00:20:52,143
Although in this case, the
facts do speak for themselves.

260
00:20:52,318 --> 00:20:53,342
One never knows.

261
00:20:55,387 --> 00:20:56,980
I know.

262
00:21:25,484 --> 00:21:27,953
Svoda, what's that?

263
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:29,440
Well, it's not in
the dictionary,

264
00:21:29,588 --> 00:21:33,548
and, uh, when I called the library,
it's not Russian or French or anything.

265
00:21:33,726 --> 00:21:36,252
- New restaurant?
- No. I checked with information.

266
00:21:36,428 --> 00:21:38,090
There's not a
business or a store

267
00:21:38,264 --> 00:21:40,426
or a restaurant of any
kind under that name.

268
00:21:40,599 --> 00:21:44,661
You know, it's written so
sloppy. Maybe it's S period Voda.

269
00:21:44,837 --> 00:21:46,237
My thoughts exactly.

270
00:21:46,405 --> 00:21:49,534
Why don't you have a
phone book and take a look?

271
00:21:49,708 --> 00:21:52,303
- Okay.
- What's in here?

272
00:21:52,478 --> 00:21:53,518
That's my spaghetti sauce.

273
00:21:53,679 --> 00:21:56,148
And there's already everything
in it and it won't blow me up.

274
00:21:56,749 --> 00:21:58,877
Won't even make you sizzle.
- Oh, dad.

275
00:22:00,619 --> 00:22:04,021
I ran into Michael Brennen
over at Carla Myers house today.

276
00:22:04,190 --> 00:22:05,488
What is he doing there?

277
00:22:05,658 --> 00:22:08,253
Oh, he's just getting a
feel for what happened.

278
00:22:08,427 --> 00:22:10,020
I thought it was
a little strange.

279
00:22:10,196 --> 00:22:13,462
So after I ran into him,
I did a little research.

280
00:22:13,632 --> 00:22:16,466
His wife was killed during
a carjacking in Chicago.

281
00:22:16,635 --> 00:22:18,866
- Did you know that?
- No.

282
00:22:19,038 --> 00:22:22,236
The day after the funeral, he
quit his practice, moved down here,

283
00:22:22,408 --> 00:22:27,540
took the bar, and started prosecuting
the bad guys with a vengeance.

284
00:22:27,713 --> 00:22:30,478
Word is, he takes every
case very personally.

285
00:22:30,649 --> 00:22:31,708
And he hates to lose.

286
00:22:33,085 --> 00:22:35,645
- I do too.
- I just think he's gonna be tough.

287
00:22:38,691 --> 00:22:40,922
- Yeah.
- What?

288
00:22:41,093 --> 00:22:45,724
S v. Oda. LEANNE: Mm?

289
00:22:46,432 --> 00:22:49,869
- State of Georgia v. Oda.
- The law case.

290
00:22:50,035 --> 00:22:53,096
Yeah. Martin Oda.

291
00:22:53,272 --> 00:22:58,210
That mobster, the one on trial for
conspiracy to commit murder last year.

292
00:22:58,377 --> 00:23:02,041
Oh, yeah, that's the case where
the key witness for the prosecution,

293
00:23:02,214 --> 00:23:05,480
- uh, what's his name, oh, uh...
- Uh, Frank, uh, Delong.

294
00:23:05,651 --> 00:23:06,675
Delong. Right.

295
00:23:06,852 --> 00:23:09,481
He disappeared from the safe
house where the feds had him stashed

296
00:23:09,655 --> 00:23:10,816
before he could testify.

297
00:23:10,990 --> 00:23:13,482
The state had to drop its
case and Oda went free.

298
00:23:13,659 --> 00:23:16,686
But what would a city
councilwoman care about that for?

299
00:23:16,862 --> 00:23:19,222
It was always rumored that
somebody in the police department

300
00:23:19,365 --> 00:23:21,960
had a hand in
Delong's disappearance.

301
00:23:22,134 --> 00:23:24,103
Maybe she was on the
verge of finding out who.

302
00:23:24,270 --> 00:23:25,932
Maybe that's why
they killed her.

303
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:30,872
You know, there was
something else about that case.

304
00:23:31,644 --> 00:23:34,637
The assistant DA who prosecuted

305
00:23:34,813 --> 00:23:38,648
was so new to the job I
didn't know who he was.

306
00:23:39,652 --> 00:23:42,884
- Not Michael Brennen?
- Heh.

307
00:23:47,226 --> 00:23:49,024
Ain't locked.

308
00:23:51,563 --> 00:23:54,362
- You got a minute?
- Sure.

309
00:23:54,533 --> 00:23:58,493
I'd offer you a, uh, hot
dog. But my burner's broken.

310
00:23:58,671 --> 00:24:02,369
Aw, I'm still full from
supper last night, argh.

311
00:24:02,541 --> 00:24:04,373
Leanne made spaghetti.

312
00:24:04,543 --> 00:24:08,036
It was okay. Didn't have
much zing to it, but it was okay.

313
00:24:08,213 --> 00:24:11,149
I understand you ran
into each other yesterday.

314
00:24:12,418 --> 00:24:15,149
Nice woman. Intelligent, pretty.
You must be very proud of her.

315
00:24:15,321 --> 00:24:16,345
Yes, I am.

316
00:24:17,823 --> 00:24:19,382
You never had any kids. Did you?

317
00:24:20,125 --> 00:24:25,029
- Heh, you been checking up on me?
- Oh, no, heh. Not really.

318
00:24:25,197 --> 00:24:27,996
I tell you what I have
been checking up on.

319
00:24:28,167 --> 00:24:30,227
The Oda case.
Remember that case?

320
00:24:32,438 --> 00:24:34,964
Of course. It was the
first one I ever prosecuted.

321
00:24:36,108 --> 00:24:39,476
- You lied?
- I did?

322
00:24:39,645 --> 00:24:41,375
You said you never lost a case.

323
00:24:43,983 --> 00:24:45,747
Heh, it was dropped.

324
00:24:45,918 --> 00:24:49,582
After we lost our star witness,
there was no longer any case to lose.

325
00:24:51,190 --> 00:24:53,091
Yeah.

326
00:24:53,258 --> 00:24:58,026
Did you ever figure out how
that fella Delong slipped away?

327
00:24:59,465 --> 00:25:00,899
No.

328
00:25:01,967 --> 00:25:03,607
But I assume your
interest in the Oda case

329
00:25:03,736 --> 00:25:06,331
goes beyond catching
me in a lie. Am I right?

330
00:25:06,505 --> 00:25:11,739
Well, Carla Myers, uh,
had a file on that case.

331
00:25:12,578 --> 00:25:14,843
Leanne found it this morning.

332
00:25:15,014 --> 00:25:17,347
She had a
transcript of the trial,

333
00:25:17,516 --> 00:25:21,351
discovery hearing, the
depositions, police reports, the works.

334
00:25:22,021 --> 00:25:25,480
- Any idea why?
- I thought maybe you might have one.

335
00:25:26,792 --> 00:25:28,192
- Me?
- Yeah.

336
00:25:28,360 --> 00:25:30,727
Well, since you prosecuted
the case, it only makes sense,

337
00:25:30,896 --> 00:25:34,264
if she had a question, she'd
come to you. Don't you think?

338
00:25:36,301 --> 00:25:40,534
- Well, she didn't.
- That's odd.

339
00:25:40,706 --> 00:25:43,175
That's odd, unless, uh, she...

340
00:25:43,342 --> 00:25:46,801
- Unless what?
- Never mind.

341
00:25:48,881 --> 00:25:52,443
I don't like insinuations,
Ben. I don't like implications.

342
00:25:52,618 --> 00:25:55,019
This is my office. If you
have something to say, say it.

343
00:25:55,854 --> 00:25:59,188
I just wondered if she
had talked to you, that's all.

344
00:26:00,492 --> 00:26:03,189
I won't take up any
more of your time.

345
00:26:04,029 --> 00:26:05,520
Thanks, Michael.

346
00:26:06,031 --> 00:26:09,195
Don't mention it,
heh. See you in court.

347
00:26:39,064 --> 00:26:41,158
Mrs. Delong, a word
with you, please.

348
00:26:41,333 --> 00:26:42,153
I'll be quick.

349
00:26:42,177 --> 00:26:44,497
I told you before
and I'll tell you again.

350
00:26:44,670 --> 00:26:47,071
I don't know where Frank is.

351
00:26:47,239 --> 00:26:49,470
Have him give me a call.

352
00:26:50,676 --> 00:26:52,838
Can't you just leave him alone?

353
00:26:54,213 --> 00:26:57,911
That's Frank Delong's wife.
She owns that beauty shop.

354
00:27:00,519 --> 00:27:03,421
Oh. He's angry. What
do you think he's saying?

355
00:27:03,589 --> 00:27:05,922
My guess is, Mr. Brennen
wants to make sure

356
00:27:06,091 --> 00:27:08,458
that Frank Delong lays very low.

357
00:27:08,627 --> 00:27:11,358
Hard for me to believe
that he had anything to do

358
00:27:11,530 --> 00:27:14,967
- with Delong's disappearance.
- Then what's he doing here?

359
00:27:19,071 --> 00:27:23,668
Maybe a real big
fish just took our bait.

360
00:27:56,575 --> 00:27:59,238
- Hello.
- Hello.

361
00:27:59,411 --> 00:28:01,312
Hello. I'm Cliff
Lewis. I'm a lawyer.

362
00:28:01,480 --> 00:28:03,278
I'm investigating the
murder of Carla Myers.

363
00:28:03,448 --> 00:28:06,179
- She lived next door?
- Oh, yes, terrible thing.

364
00:28:06,351 --> 00:28:08,183
- I did her yard.
- Really?

365
00:28:08,353 --> 00:28:11,687
After I did this one,
and before I did that one.

366
00:28:12,391 --> 00:28:15,828
- Uh, were you here last Tuesday?
- Every Tuesday.

367
00:28:16,428 --> 00:28:18,388
About the same time of
day, late in the afternoon?

368
00:28:18,530 --> 00:28:20,931
Sometimes very late. Yes.

369
00:28:22,801 --> 00:28:24,497
Let me ask you a question.

370
00:28:26,972 --> 00:28:31,672
- Well, what exactly did he say?
- Nothing. That's the whole point.

371
00:28:31,843 --> 00:28:34,472
The gardener was doing the
lawn next to Carla Myers house,

372
00:28:34,646 --> 00:28:37,047
and he's prepared
to testify that nobody,

373
00:28:37,216 --> 00:28:38,514
not to mention Arthur Phillips,

374
00:28:38,684 --> 00:28:41,882
came running out of that
house the day of the murder.

375
00:28:42,054 --> 00:28:46,754
- Bus driver's lying, Jerry.
- Well, heh, somebody sure is.

376
00:28:46,925 --> 00:28:50,487
And what's more, it's looking to
me like the Carla Myers murder

377
00:28:50,662 --> 00:28:52,422
had something to do
with the Martin Oda case.

378
00:28:52,564 --> 00:28:53,862
The Oda case?

379
00:28:54,032 --> 00:28:56,194
Since Michael Brennen was
the prosecutor on that one,

380
00:28:56,368 --> 00:28:59,304
we might very well be looking
at a conflict of interest here.

381
00:29:04,576 --> 00:29:08,308
Uh, Michael, come upstairs for a
few minutes. Would you, please?

382
00:29:12,784 --> 00:29:16,585
God help you if this is
some kind of trick, Ben.

383
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:19,816
Heh, are you serious?

384
00:29:20,592 --> 00:29:22,220
Is he serious?

385
00:29:22,394 --> 00:29:24,761
Are you suggesting
that I killed Carla Myers?

386
00:29:24,930 --> 00:29:26,796
I'm saying your
star witness is lying

387
00:29:26,965 --> 00:29:29,434
and maybe you shouldn't be
prosecuting this case. That's all.

388
00:29:29,601 --> 00:29:32,969
Ben, I don't mean to be rude. But
I don't really care what you think.

389
00:29:34,106 --> 00:29:36,666
Why were you with Frank
Delong's wife yesterday?

390
00:29:41,179 --> 00:29:42,477
You followed me?

391
00:29:44,249 --> 00:29:47,686
- You mean it's true?
- Yeah.

392
00:29:49,521 --> 00:29:51,649
I talked to her.

393
00:29:53,225 --> 00:29:55,696
If this is for real,
if you're really

394
00:29:55,720 --> 00:29:58,687
trying to pin this murder
on me, hang it up.

395
00:29:58,864 --> 00:30:01,800
If you keep shaking this tree, you're
not gonna like what falls out of it.

396
00:30:01,967 --> 00:30:04,698
Just trying to keep you
abreast of the facts, Jerry.

397
00:30:06,271 --> 00:30:08,069
What you do with
them is up to you.

398
00:30:13,812 --> 00:30:16,680
What were you
doing out at Delong's?

399
00:30:18,116 --> 00:30:21,177
That case has
always bothered me.

400
00:30:21,353 --> 00:30:23,379
And after Matlock
came in yesterday

401
00:30:23,555 --> 00:30:26,684
waving it in my face, it started
bothering me even more.

402
00:30:26,858 --> 00:30:29,020
So I went over and
tried to get Mrs. Delong

403
00:30:29,194 --> 00:30:33,097
to tell me where Frank was
and who helped him fly the coop.

404
00:30:33,632 --> 00:30:35,294
Only she wouldn't talk.

405
00:30:37,102 --> 00:30:39,697
Is there something you
wanna tell me, Michael?

406
00:30:43,041 --> 00:30:44,771
No.

407
00:31:11,903 --> 00:31:17,171
Hello, Al, 6:16 on the button.
You do run a tight ship. Don't you?

408
00:31:17,342 --> 00:31:19,062
Hello, Mr. Brennen.
What are you doing here?

409
00:31:19,211 --> 00:31:23,205
I'm going over to the Myers
house. I don't feel like driving.

410
00:31:23,382 --> 00:31:25,078
Oh, forget it, Mr. Brennen.

411
00:31:25,250 --> 00:31:26,843
Now, now, now, Al,

412
00:31:27,018 --> 00:31:30,716
we city officials have to be
above reproach. Above reproach.

413
00:31:30,889 --> 00:31:33,984
- Am I right, Al?
- That's right, Mr. Brennen.

414
00:31:44,403 --> 00:31:47,373
Were any of these passengers
on the bus the day of the murder?

415
00:31:47,539 --> 00:31:50,771
Uh, no. I don't think so.

416
00:31:50,942 --> 00:31:52,103
Is something wrong?

417
00:31:53,311 --> 00:31:55,542
Ah, no. Not really.

418
00:31:55,714 --> 00:31:57,774
Matlock has come up
with someone who says

419
00:31:57,949 --> 00:32:01,477
nobody came running out
of the Myers house at 6:18.

420
00:32:02,821 --> 00:32:05,882
Don't worry about
it. I'll prove he's a liar.

421
00:32:26,578 --> 00:32:29,810
Mr. White. Ben Matlock,
we met in the DA's office.

422
00:32:29,981 --> 00:32:32,576
Your wife said you might
be here. How about it?

423
00:32:32,751 --> 00:32:34,391
- Uh, sure. Help yourself.
- There you go.

424
00:32:41,393 --> 00:32:43,191
- Tough day, huh?
- Yeah.

425
00:32:43,361 --> 00:32:46,354
- Yeah. They're all tough.
- Your wife sure is in a good mood.

426
00:32:46,531 --> 00:32:50,764
She's all atwitter about the
roses you got her. Three dozen.

427
00:32:50,936 --> 00:32:54,134
A hundred and eighty dollars
plus tax. That's really something.

428
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:57,275
Yeah. So is my
wife. She deserves it.

429
00:32:57,442 --> 00:32:59,741
- Miss? WOMAN:
Yes, sir. Just a minute.

430
00:32:59,911 --> 00:33:02,176
She has no idea where
you got the money.

431
00:33:02,347 --> 00:33:04,748
She figures maybe you got
some kind of bonus or something

432
00:33:04,916 --> 00:33:08,353
- and forgot to tell her about it.
- Yeah. Something like that.

433
00:33:08,520 --> 00:33:11,490
Michael Brennen tell
you about the gardener?

434
00:33:12,457 --> 00:33:13,550
What gardener?

435
00:33:13,725 --> 00:33:16,752
The one who's working in the
yard next to Carla Myers' house

436
00:33:16,928 --> 00:33:18,453
the day she was murdered.

437
00:33:18,630 --> 00:33:20,997
He said nobody came out of
that house, running or otherwise.

438
00:33:21,166 --> 00:33:24,398
He was there the whole
time. Real good witness too.

439
00:33:24,569 --> 00:33:26,902
Mr. Brennen couldn't shake
him when he took his deposition,

440
00:33:27,072 --> 00:33:28,768
and believe me, he tried.

441
00:33:29,708 --> 00:33:32,268
Um, service here is a joke.

442
00:33:32,444 --> 00:33:33,955
I'm gonna go to the
bar, get another beer.

443
00:33:33,979 --> 00:33:35,971
- Can I get you anything?
- No, no.

444
00:34:06,144 --> 00:34:07,635
Ain't locked.

445
00:34:09,047 --> 00:34:11,812
No need to vacuum. Just
get the waste baskets. Okay?

446
00:34:12,951 --> 00:34:14,943
I don't do waste baskets.

447
00:34:16,087 --> 00:34:20,548
- Burner fixed?
- Yeah. But the kitchen's closed.

448
00:34:21,159 --> 00:34:24,095
Al Weiss got your phone
number in his little black book.

449
00:34:24,796 --> 00:34:25,889
Excuse me?

450
00:34:26,064 --> 00:34:31,526
The bus driver has this phone
number in his address book. No name.

451
00:34:31,703 --> 00:34:33,968
I guess he doesn't
want people to find out

452
00:34:34,139 --> 00:34:36,608
that you and he are
close personal friends.

453
00:34:38,076 --> 00:34:39,908
I didn't give him
my phone number.

454
00:34:40,078 --> 00:34:42,775
I saw it with my own eyes,
which pretty much confirms

455
00:34:42,948 --> 00:34:44,507
what I've been
thinking all along.

456
00:34:44,683 --> 00:34:45,844
Which was what?

457
00:34:46,017 --> 00:34:49,784
That you paid him to lie
about seeing my client that day

458
00:34:49,955 --> 00:34:52,220
so the real killer
could go free.

459
00:34:53,058 --> 00:34:56,893
- I think you should leave.
- Who killed her, anyway? The mob?

460
00:34:57,495 --> 00:35:01,159
For all I know, you put
that phone number in there.

461
00:35:01,333 --> 00:35:03,053
Why don't you just
get the hell out of here?

462
00:35:03,201 --> 00:35:06,228
Why don't you ask old Al
where he got that number?

463
00:35:06,404 --> 00:35:08,999
- You can bet I will.
- You don't scare me.

464
00:35:09,174 --> 00:35:12,702
I'm not trying to.
I'm saving that for Al.

465
00:35:12,877 --> 00:35:15,676
That's the problem with people
who know they're committing perjury.

466
00:35:15,847 --> 00:35:17,907
They scare real easy.

467
00:35:22,354 --> 00:35:24,789
When I get on with old Al,

468
00:35:24,956 --> 00:35:28,256
they're gonna have to get
him up off that stand with a mop.

469
00:35:30,795 --> 00:35:32,024
Hmm.

470
00:36:30,488 --> 00:36:34,721
It's Brennen. We need to talk.

471
00:36:50,675 --> 00:36:53,035
Ladies and gentlemen,
you're about to hear evidence

472
00:36:53,178 --> 00:36:55,875
that is clear and conclusive.

473
00:36:56,047 --> 00:36:59,142
It will prove beyond
the shadow of a doubt

474
00:36:59,317 --> 00:37:05,086
that that man over there, Arthur
Phillips, murdered Carla Myers.

475
00:37:05,757 --> 00:37:08,693
His fingerprints were
found at the scene.

476
00:37:08,860 --> 00:37:13,958
The watch that she always
wore was found in his garage.

477
00:37:14,466 --> 00:37:19,029
A man saw him fleeing her
house at the time of the murder.

478
00:37:20,305 --> 00:37:25,505
You'll also hear why Arthur
Phillips murdered Carla Myers.

479
00:37:27,412 --> 00:37:31,042
You'll hear how she
as a public servant,

480
00:37:31,216 --> 00:37:32,912
looking out for the public good,

481
00:37:33,084 --> 00:37:37,613
that very day exposed
him as an embezzler.

482
00:37:37,789 --> 00:37:42,727
You will hear how
he, his life ruined,

483
00:37:42,894 --> 00:37:47,298
nothing to live for,
murdered her out of revenge,

484
00:37:47,465 --> 00:37:51,766
then tried to make what he had
done look like a burglary gone awry.

485
00:37:53,571 --> 00:37:59,238
That's right, ladies and gentlemen.
You will hear all these things.

486
00:38:00,078 --> 00:38:03,674
And you will weigh
all these things

487
00:38:04,382 --> 00:38:11,084
and they will convince you that
Arthur Phillips is guilty of murder.

488
00:38:11,956 --> 00:38:15,290
The problem is, you'll be wrong.

489
00:38:16,928 --> 00:38:22,060
Order. Order. Order.

490
00:38:22,233 --> 00:38:24,065
Your Honor, I need to
see you in chambers.

491
00:38:24,569 --> 00:38:26,197
By all means.

492
00:38:26,371 --> 00:38:30,172
Mr. Matlock, you too.
This court is in recess.

493
00:38:33,912 --> 00:38:37,405
Jerry. Good. Have a seat.

494
00:38:38,449 --> 00:38:39,889
And when you're
good and comfortable,

495
00:38:40,051 --> 00:38:41,713
you can tell me what's going on.

496
00:38:43,221 --> 00:38:45,520
- What do you mean? MICHAEL:
He didn't tell me to do that.

497
00:38:45,690 --> 00:38:46,817
My conscience did.

498
00:38:47,392 --> 00:38:50,055
You as much as
told that jury out there

499
00:38:50,228 --> 00:38:52,561
that you think the
defendant is innocent.

500
00:38:52,730 --> 00:38:55,928
- What?
- On my 25 years on the bench...

501
00:38:56,100 --> 00:38:59,002
this is a first for
me, Mr. Brennen.

502
00:38:59,170 --> 00:39:02,299
Frankly, I'm not sure whether
I should declare a mistrial,

503
00:39:02,473 --> 00:39:06,433
or slap you with a
contempt citation or both.

504
00:39:06,611 --> 00:39:08,603
He's hoping for a
mistrial, Your Honor.

505
00:39:08,780 --> 00:39:12,410
- Myself, I like that slapping part.
- Shut up.

506
00:39:12,584 --> 00:39:14,052
Take it easy, Mr. Brennen.

507
00:39:16,087 --> 00:39:17,419
What do you know about this?

508
00:39:17,589 --> 00:39:21,458
I know that this case is somehow
all tangled up with the Oda case.

509
00:39:21,626 --> 00:39:23,595
Mr. Brennen knows that too.

510
00:39:23,761 --> 00:39:26,162
He also knows that I'm
gonna keep looking into it

511
00:39:26,331 --> 00:39:29,426
as long as my client's on
trial. He doesn't want that.

512
00:39:29,601 --> 00:39:33,003
- So he's throwing this trial.
- That's a lie.

513
00:39:33,571 --> 00:39:38,373
Your fair-haired boy needs a
bath, Jerry. He's gotten real dirty.

514
00:39:38,543 --> 00:39:42,002
All right. That's
it. You white trash.

515
00:39:42,180 --> 00:39:46,379
- What did you call me?
- Nobody accuses me of being dirty.

516
00:39:46,551 --> 00:39:48,671
- Dad, sit down.
- If the shoe fits, wear it and weep.

517
00:39:48,820 --> 00:39:50,686
Dad. JUDGE: All right. Enough.

518
00:39:50,855 --> 00:39:55,657
Everybody, sit down
and shut up. Now.

519
00:39:59,063 --> 00:40:03,330
Any more outbursts like that
and I'll throw the lot of you into jail.

520
00:40:06,170 --> 00:40:08,639
Okay. Where were we?

521
00:40:13,544 --> 00:40:16,104
I've been looking into the
Oda case too, Your Honor,

522
00:40:16,281 --> 00:40:20,912
not for any devious personal
reasons but because I want the truth.

523
00:40:21,386 --> 00:40:24,049
I also feel that there is,
there's some connection

524
00:40:24,222 --> 00:40:26,953
between this case and what
happened to Frank Delong

525
00:40:27,125 --> 00:40:28,889
and the Oda case.

526
00:40:29,560 --> 00:40:32,257
And that's the real reason
that Carla Myers was murdered.

527
00:40:33,064 --> 00:40:37,525
I contacted Mrs. Delong and
she in turn contacted Frank.

528
00:40:37,702 --> 00:40:42,402
He's agreed to turn
himself in to the FBI.

529
00:40:42,573 --> 00:40:44,542
- When?
- Tonight.

530
00:40:47,245 --> 00:40:51,114
She's going to pick him up and
drive him to FBI headquarters,

531
00:40:51,282 --> 00:40:53,251
where I intend
to interrogate him

532
00:40:53,418 --> 00:40:56,115
until he tells me who let
him out of that safe house.

533
00:40:56,854 --> 00:40:58,755
What's the bottom
line, Mr. Brennen?

534
00:40:59,457 --> 00:41:01,585
I'd like you to declare
a 48-hour recess.

535
00:41:01,759 --> 00:41:04,228
He's up to something, Your Honor.
- Dad.

536
00:41:04,395 --> 00:41:07,763
The truth in that case will
lead us to the truth in this case.

537
00:41:07,932 --> 00:41:09,366
I'm sure of it.

538
00:41:14,038 --> 00:41:15,131
Very well.

539
00:43:10,721 --> 00:43:12,383
Hey, aw.

540
00:43:49,427 --> 00:43:50,588
Hello, Jerry.

541
00:43:51,662 --> 00:43:52,857
All right. Drop it.

542
00:43:59,470 --> 00:44:01,098
Okay, Phil.

543
00:44:05,076 --> 00:44:07,443
Oh, Jerry, Jerry.

544
00:44:07,612 --> 00:44:10,776
You were gonna kill Frank Delong
just like you killed Carla Myers.

545
00:44:10,948 --> 00:44:12,746
You'll have a hard
time proving that.

546
00:44:12,917 --> 00:44:14,579
I don't know.

547
00:44:14,752 --> 00:44:16,084
Now that we know that it's you,

548
00:44:16,254 --> 00:44:19,122
I think the police will be able to
place you at the Myers' house.

549
00:44:19,290 --> 00:44:21,020
- You agree, Bobby?
- Uh-huh.

550
00:44:21,192 --> 00:44:25,527
After that, it's only a matter of time
before they can prove you're a killer.

551
00:44:27,231 --> 00:44:29,291
What happened to you?

552
00:44:30,601 --> 00:44:33,298
You betrayed everything
that you believed in,

553
00:44:33,471 --> 00:44:35,235
the law, the
people, your office,

554
00:44:35,406 --> 00:44:37,807
everything that I
thought you cared about.

555
00:44:37,975 --> 00:44:39,705
Why'd you do it,
Jerry? The money?

556
00:44:41,712 --> 00:44:42,736
Let's go.

557
00:44:44,415 --> 00:44:46,543
The money mean that much to you?

558
00:44:46,717 --> 00:44:48,413
Make sure you read
him his rights, Bobby.

559
00:44:48,586 --> 00:44:54,526
I want everything by the book
so I can bury him fair and square.

560
00:44:59,096 --> 00:45:02,191
Vienna natural casing hot
dogs and Mary Ann buns.

561
00:45:02,366 --> 00:45:05,097
- Onions.
- Onions. How many?

562
00:45:05,269 --> 00:45:07,602
This many onions.

563
00:45:07,772 --> 00:45:11,004
- He cut two onions.
- Thank you, Father.

564
00:45:11,175 --> 00:45:15,112
Okay. Two onions. Six dogs.
That's a third of an onion per dog.

565
00:45:15,279 --> 00:45:17,145
Were they small,
medium or large onions?

566
00:45:17,315 --> 00:45:18,783
I don't know. They're onions.

567
00:45:18,950 --> 00:45:20,790
I hate to interrupt the
afternoon cooking show.

568
00:45:20,952 --> 00:45:23,752
But I have a question about the
case. How did you know it was Carlisle?

569
00:45:23,921 --> 00:45:26,041
- You didn't tell him?
- Well, I was sworn to secrecy.

570
00:45:26,190 --> 00:45:27,522
I couldn't tell anybody but you.

571
00:45:29,193 --> 00:45:31,958
Ben saw my phone number
in Al White's address book.

572
00:45:32,129 --> 00:45:35,190
You see, up until three months
ago, my office was Jerry's,

573
00:45:35,366 --> 00:45:36,886
and the phone number
was never changed.

574
00:45:37,034 --> 00:45:38,127
Oh, I see.

575
00:45:38,302 --> 00:45:41,238
That way you knew that he knew Al.
- Yeah.

576
00:45:41,405 --> 00:45:43,125
I realized he could
have easily been the one

577
00:45:43,274 --> 00:45:45,072
who snuck Delong
out of that safe house.

578
00:45:45,243 --> 00:45:47,371
So I called Ben.

579
00:45:47,545 --> 00:45:49,810
And we staged a scene in
Judge Caldwell's chambers

580
00:45:49,981 --> 00:45:52,507
to trick him into
tipping his hand.

581
00:45:52,683 --> 00:45:54,683
Now, we found his account
in the Bahamas yesterday,

582
00:45:54,852 --> 00:45:57,879
three hundred and
fifty thousand big ones.

583
00:45:58,055 --> 00:46:00,456
- Ooh. MATLOCK:
Come and get them.

584
00:46:00,625 --> 00:46:02,617
Hold it. Don't forget these.
- Huh?

585
00:46:02,793 --> 00:46:09,393
- Tiny, hot sport peppers to taste.
- Tiny, hot sport peppers to taste.

586
00:46:09,567 --> 00:46:12,002
By the way, Michael, your
performance in the judge's chambers

587
00:46:12,169 --> 00:46:14,502
the other day was
excellent. Very believable.

588
00:46:14,672 --> 00:46:17,301
- Why, thank you.
- You don't think I was believable?

589
00:46:17,475 --> 00:46:20,707
Well, actually, dad, you
were a little over the top.

590
00:46:20,878 --> 00:46:22,870
I wasn't over the top.

591
00:46:23,047 --> 00:46:25,607
It's okay, pop, you just
tend to overdo things.

592
00:46:25,783 --> 00:46:28,048
Well, cheers, everybody.

593
00:46:28,419 --> 00:46:30,888
Well, wait, you gotta
have a couple of these.

594
00:46:31,055 --> 00:46:35,322
- They're not too hot?
- No. They won't blow you up.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
